    <div class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900 mb-3">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I find the exact point?</summary>
        <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Each guide gives precise landmarks. Confirm you have the right spot with three cues: the anatomical description, the tenderness test (the correct point feels achy or different from surrounding tissue), and the depression sign (most points sit in a slight hollow).</div>
      </details>
      <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900 mb-3">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How hard should I press?</summary>
        <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Enough to feel a deep, dull ache — the deqi sensation — but never sharp, shooting, or radiating pain. Build the pressure gradually over 10-15 seconds and ease off if it becomes painful.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900 mb-3">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Which points should I avoid during pregnancy?</summary>
        <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">LI4 (Hegu), SP6 (Sanyinjiao), BL60 (Kunlun), and GB21 (Jianjing) are traditionally avoided during pregnancy because they are believed to stimulate contractions. PC6 (Neiguan) is commonly used for morning sickness, but always check with your healthcare provider first.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I use these points on myself?</summary>
        <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. Most points on the hands, arms, legs, face, and torso are accessible with your own fingers. Points on the back may require a massage tool, an acupressure mat, or a partner.</div>
      </details>
